658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f36f74d-9ded-58b3-8942-de350905b5b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac9732d3-1086-5e0e-8f66-b1641de82b66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315083 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-3x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{3}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{3}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2+\\frac{3}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)(-1)^{n}-2)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2).\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379595 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in [0,12),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-12,12).$ What is the value of $f(-4)?$\n\n$f(-4)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0188791e-a7fa-5d1c-b905-419c47766caf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379595 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,12),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,12), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-12,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-12,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-6(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-(-4)^{2}-6(-4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379596 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379596 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379596 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186379597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379596 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "62443fee-fe96-5815-bb89-8967be450b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379597 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "669c79c2-1922-5d4f-8294-a85dd5e7d7f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
